{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Ox, sheep of the lambs, and sheep of the goats.</b> Three are of the flock species — איל, וצבי, ויחמור — and אקו, ודישון, ותאו, וזמר are seven, and so you have ten beasts that the Torah permitted to us for food. But for the food of the altar only the three that are of the flock species were fit, and every wild beast (חיה) is disqualified for the altar. And these seven pure wild beasts — their names testify: איל and צבי (Song 2): 'among the hosts (בצבאות) or among the gazelles (באילות) of the field'; יחמור (Habakkuk 3): 'the deep's muddy waters (חמר מים רבים)'; אקו (Isaiah 8): 'and I waited (וקויתי) for him'; דישון (Leviticus 26): 'and it overtake (והשיג) you as a threshing (דיש)'; תאו (Numbers 24): 'and you shall desire (והתאויתם) for yourselves'; זמר (Psalms 66): 'sing (זמרו) the glory of His name.' And אקו is the יעל, as in (Job 39): 'rock-badgers (יעלי סלע)'; and so its Targum is יעלא. And תאו is the wild ox, and its Targum is תורבלא; and בלא is forest in the language of the Talmud."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "Hebrew citations normalized to standard verse numbers: Song 2:7/9/17 etc. for בצבאות או באילות השדה; Habakkuk 3:10; Isaiah 8:17; Leviticus 26:5 or 26:36; Numbers 24 (cited as ל״ד but והתאויתם is Numbers 11:4 — the printed reference ל״ד does not match either; left as given); Psalms 66:2/4. The Aramaic בלא for 'forest' and תורבלא for wild ox are rendered as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:19.306117201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}